{"agent_id":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","agent_seq":22,"timestamp":"2026-08-06T02:51:31.101517856Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"05129aa4-5ab4-4e79-a886-1e8ffe0a420b","comment_uuid":"212a2b73-4f74-4226-87a7-02c6e3807e35","author":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","content":"## Review Summary: PASS (deliverable claims corroborated) — with a blocking limitation on live re-run\n\nRole: independent adversarial review, big-pickle. Read-only. Reviewed #196 deliverable at research/toolregistry-lazy-mcp/ (commit ebe07b3) plus the installed venv at /tmp/toolregistry-venv.\n\n### OBSERVATION — what I ran and what happened\n\n1. Read all deliverable files in full: report.md, proxy.py, backend.py, manifest.json, capture_manifest.py, tests/run_tests.py + common.py + phase123.py + phase4.py + phase5.py + baseline_always_on.py, and all 7 logs.\n2. Re-derived EVERY timing claim from raw epoch_ms fields (cross-process, same-host wall clock):\n   - Test 1 registration 2807ms: 1785983152554-1785983149747=2807 ✓; proxy served initialize->tools/list->close (rel 29ms) with ZERO backend_spawn_start and backend_pid_file_exists=False ✓; REGISTRY| dump matches manifest.json (add, a/b number, required [a,b]) ✓\n   - Test 2 invocation->backend_up 2614ms (1785983155841-1785983153227=2614) ✓; backend_up->response 10ms (851-841) ✓; end-to-end 2639ms (866-227) ✓; decomposition proxy-spawn 1342ms (569-227) ✓, handshake->call 16ms (585-569) ✓, backend init 1255.5ms (init_ms field) ✓, forward+response 10ms ✓\n   - Test 3 steady state 14/17/7ms (CALL delta_ms) ✓; single instance be-1-1497407-54585 across all 4 forwards, spawn count 1 ✓\n   - Test 4 kill detection 20ms ✓; second call end-to-end 1587ms (1785983165069-1785983163482=1587) ✓; failure in SAME millisecond as request (both 3485) ✓; respawn init 1573.6ms ✓; third call 10ms, spawn count stays 2, pid 1497595 reused ✓\n   - Test 5 register 2665ms ✓; zero spawns at register ✓; notification_related_lines are only startup marker notifications=never + RECEIVED notifications/initialized (client->server handshake) — zero outgoing notifications ✓\n   - Baseline register 2786ms ✓; first call 1453ms ✓; steady state 5/9/3/5ms ✓\n3. Verified every source citation against the INSTALLED venv (toolregistry 0.15.0, mcp 2.0.0, python 3.10):\n   - grep list_changed across toolregistry/ incl. _vendor/: ZERO matches ✓\n   - integrations/mcp/client.py:13 imports ClientSession, StdioServerParameters ✓; ToolRegistry's MCPClient is a thin wrapper over ClientSession only (never the high-level mcp.client.Client) ✓\n   - mcp/client/session.py:1080-1088: validate_tool_result -> if name not in _tool_output_schemas: await self.list_tools() (cache-miss refresh) ✓; session.py:1289 send_roots_list_changed is ROOTS only ✓\n   - mcp/client/client.py:688/691: high-level Client.listen(tools_list_changed=False default) exists but unused by ToolRegistry ✓\n   - mcp/client/stdio.py:56 inherited env list HOME/LOGNAME/PATH/SHELL/TERM/USER; :75 get_default_environment; :128 env merge ✓\n   - integrations/mcp/connection.py: MCPConnectionManager._client starts None (lazy), _call_persistent catches Exception -> _connect (recreate MCPClient) -> retry once ✓ (report cited :106)\n   - _mixins/registration.py:90 def register_from_mcp(transport, namespace, persistent=True, headers, **kwargs) ✓; METADATA: mcp<3,>=1.24.0 + httpx>=0.28.1 extra == mcp ✓\n   - tool_registry.py: __repr__ (177/183) and __str__ (185/191) both return json.dumps(get_schemas(), indent=2); print(registry) uses __str__ ✓\n   - mcp/server/lowlevel/server.py:107-111: NotificationOptions defaults tools_changed=False, so create_initialization_options() with no args does NOT advertise tools/list_changed ✓\n4. Code review of proxy.py/backend.py/tests: cached tools/list from manifest (209-211), spawn-on-first-call (221), one reused session, respawn-once-and-retry (168-192), zero notification-send code paths, stateless across processes (manifest read from disk) ✓. Pass criteria in all 5 phases are soundly computed from observed artifacts (proxy log events, pid files, results) ✓.\n5. LIVE RE-RUN ATTEMPT: BLOCKED. /tmp/toolregistry-venv/bin/python tests/run_tests.py (and every python invocation) is denied by the reviewer role's OpenCode permission map (.opencode/agents/reviewer.md:7-16), which allows only crosslink/opencode/git/ls/cat/cargo/npm/rtk. hook-config.json:150 allowed_bash_prefixes DOES include 'python ' (crosslink-guard would allow it), so this is a config gap between the plugin allowlist and the agent definition — not a deliverable defect. Intervention logged (tool_blocked). No alternative execution route was used (npm/cargo/python bypass would violate AGENTS.md failure-handling: no substitute strategies without approval).\n\n### FINDINGS — what this implies for the report's verdicts\n\n- F1 (MUST FIX — for THIS REVIEW, not the deliverable): the distinguishing mandate (independent fresh-run re-measurement, tests 1-4 live) was NOT executable in this sandbox. The report's numbers are corroborated by (a) full arithmetic re-derivation from raw multi-process epoch logs whose PIDs/timestamps are internally consistent across harness+proxy+backend captures, and (b) source-level verification of every claim. They are NOT freshly re-measured. If independent live numbers are required for the cold-start ceiling, a builder-role agent or the operator must run /tmp/toolregistry-venv/bin/python tests/run_tests.py once, or reviewer bash must gain 'python ' (already present in hook-config allowed_bash_prefixes).\n- F2: All five PASS verdicts and the §7 conclusion ARE supported by the recorded evidence. Every observation row in §4 maps to a log line I re-derived. The conclusion's two caveats (proxy spawned twice, stateless across processes; cold start ~2.6s = two subprocess startups, steady-state ~5-10ms) are directly evidenced.\n- F3 (SHOULD CONSIDER): Test 1's automated manifest-match check is only a substring test ('add' in schema_dump and manifest) — weaker than the report's prose claim of schema identity. The dumped REGISTRY| block does substantively match manifest.json, so the evidence supports the claim; a deep compare (json equal) would make the harness criterion match the claim.\n- F4 (SHOULD CONSIDER): §5 decomposition sums to ~2623.5ms vs the 2639ms end-to-end; the ~15ms residual is the SDK's post-call tools/list refresh (disclosed in §6 item 2, but not reconciled in the timing table). Recomputed: response 851 -> tools/list request 864 -> harness end 866. Minor clarity issue only.\n- F5 (SHOULD CONSIDER): single-run measurements — the report explicitly flags them as indicative (§5), which is the correct epistemic framing for a cold-start ceiling input.\n- F6 (NIT): report cites tool_registry.py:185 for the json.dumps(get_schemas(), indent=2) print behavior; 185 is the def __str__ line (return at 191; __repr__ return at 183). Citation is essentially correct for print() semantics.\n- F7 (NIT): Test 4 operationalization deviation (§3.1) is disclosed and justified — verified that MCPConnectionManager creates the persistent connection lazily on first call, so 'kill between register and first call' is literally impossible without persistent=False. Faithful operationalization, honestly documented.\n\n### NOTABLE POSITIVES\n\n- Observations/findings separation is disciplined throughout; every claim carries a log line or file reference.\n- The operationalization deviation for Test 4 is disclosed rather than papered over.\n- Unanticipated behaviours (§6) — double proxy spawn, SDK post-call tools/list, stdio env filtering, ToolRegistry's own reconnect-retry, lazy persistent connection — are genuine, source-verified discoveries that materially inform the identifier-first proposal.\n- Harness instrumentation (epoch_ms cross-process timestamps, pid files, per-phase logs) is exactly what makes independent re-derivation possible.\n- Scope discipline h